  Now I have everything I need. Let me also do the occupancy arithmetic cleanly:

  Given: integer wall 6:21 = 381 s, OpenMS wall 6:54 = 414 s. Window-loop occupancy 68.5x vs 81.0x (CPU-s per wall-s of the window loop). CPU totals: 14,249 vs 19,440 (I'll treat as whole-run CPU-s; prompt says "27% less CPU work" — 14249/19440 = 0.733, yes -27%).

  Occupancy ratio: 68.5/81.0 = 0.8457. Work ratio: 0.7330. For occupancy to be EQUAL with no scheduling defect, wall would have to shrink by the full work ratio: 0.733. Occupancy ratio = work ratio / loop-wall ratio → loop-wall ratio = 0.7330/0.8457 = 0.867.

  So the integer window loop needs to be ~13% shorter in wall for full consistency. Overall wall ratio: 381/414 = 0.920. Decompose: wall = T_fixed + W_loop. OpenMS: 414 = T + W. Integer: 381 = T + 0.867W. Subtract: 33 = 0.133W → W = 248 s, T = 166 s. Integer loop = 215 s. Check CPU: if the cited CPU-s are whole-run, loop CPU isn't directly given; if they were loop CPU: 68.5 × 215 = 14,728 vs 14,249 (-3.3%); 81.0 × 248 = 20,088 vs 19,440 (-3.3%). The symmetric 3.3% suggests the cited CPU-s are whole-run and ~3.3% of CPU sits outside the loop (load/MS1), which fits: T=166 s of non-loop at ~1x occupancy ≈ 500 CPU-s… 19440-20088 = -648, i.e. predicted loop CPU exceeds total by 648 s — consistent with ~648 CPU-s outside the loop. So fully consistent within ~3%.

  Residual: occupancy ratio 0.846 vs work ratio 0.733 → the integer path gets 15% LESS occupancy per unit work... another way: if scheduling were perfect (occupancy equal), integer loop wall would be 14249/81 = 176 s instead of 215 s → 39 s, ~10% of total wall. So the residual is real but bounded at ~39 s (≈10% of wall, ~18% of loop wall), NOT the 20-point occupancy-gap reading. And the direction note from the plan: stalled-on-memory threads still count as occupied, so bandwidth can't explain lower occupancy with lower CPU. What's worth measuring: the free 1 Hz thread-state sampler (plan §5), a split-stage timer (E6 prerequisite — the split is inside t_trace), and libgomp task-idle behavior at window tails.

